\title{Strong induction}
\date{2026-04-01T00:22:19Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Sometimes it’s difficult—or even impossible—to directly prove #{P(k+1)} from the assumption of #{P(k)}. For example, what if we can’t prove #{P(4)} from #{P(3)}, but we can prove it from #{P(2)}? Is that OK? What if we can only prove #{P(3)} by assuming \em{both} #{P(0)} and #{P(1)}? Is that OK?}

\p{In general, yes, these are OK: as long as we can prove each proposition from assuming only \em{previous} propositions, we will eventually be able to prove any of them. This leads to the principle of \em{strong} induction. Instead of assuming only #{P(k)} as our induction hypothesis and then proving #{P(k+1)}, like this: ##{P(k)
  \implies P(k+1)} we will instaed assume \em{all} the previous propositions and use them to prove #{P(k+1)}, like this: ##{\big(P(0) \land P(1) \land P(2) \land \dots \land P(k) \big)
  \implies P(k+1).} Written out, a first take on the principle of strong induction thus looks like this: ##{P(0) \land (\forall k \in \N \st \big(P(0) \land P(1) \land \dots \land
  P(k)\big) \implies P(k+1)) \implies (\forall n \in \N \st P(n)).} Alternatively, we could write it without the #{\dots} like this: ##{P(0) \land (\forall k \in \N \st [\all{j}{\N}(j \leq k) \implies P(j)]
  \implies P(k+1)) \implies (\forall n \in \N. P(n)).}}
\p{We can also make this a bit more general, in two ways:}
\ol{\li{\p{Just like in the [example proving #{2^n < n!}](00CJ), the base case need not actually be #{0}.}
}
\li{\p{Sometimes the inductive step might need a certain number of previous cases to “get off the ground”. In this case we need more base cases to start.}
}
}

\put\transclude/expanded{false}
\transclude{00CN}

\put\transclude/expanded{true}
\transclude{00CO}

\put\transclude/expanded{false}
\transclude{00CP}

\put\transclude/expanded{true}
\transclude{00CQ}
